00:45:43.120 --> 00:45:45.640
Well, no, and I don't know that anybody really thinks it is,

00:45:45.740 --> 00:45:50.780
but they will fade quietly tonight and go home for an early bath and say we were the fastest down the Strait.

00:45:50.960 --> 00:45:52.460
Yeah, feel pleased about it.

00:45:53.680 --> 00:45:59.550
Rather a pity when you think of the late great challenges here at Le Mans, at Renault, at Matra, and of course...

00:46:00.600 --> 00:46:02.750
Dele, Dele, years ago.

00:46:05.770 --> 00:46:09.290
This race has alternated very much, really, between the nations, hasn't it?

00:46:09.640 --> 00:46:11.700
Oh, yes, I mean, going back to its very inception,

00:46:12.230 --> 00:46:13.770
the British were in from the word go,

00:46:13.830 --> 00:46:16.050
and it wasn't very much after that, of course,

00:46:16.130 --> 00:46:18.110
that the Germans joined in, and the Americans, too,

00:46:18.610 --> 00:46:23.260
with the black horse, Hawk Stutz, and the Mercedes, and things like this,

00:46:23.630 --> 00:46:26.320
which faced the tremendous effort of Bentley,

00:46:26.480 --> 00:46:29.500
and that was, of course, in the 20s, right up to 1930, their last win.

00:46:30.220 --> 00:46:46.970
Over the year, say, the Bentleys and the Jaguars, Aston Martins from England, you've had the Mercedes and the Porsches, particularly from Germany, you've had the Fords from America, and you've had the Italian Ferraris and Alphas.

00:46:47.130 --> 00:46:59.530
It's been very much an international event for many, many years. Huge prestige, of course, always, and commercial result for the manufacturer that wins. That is another works Porsche stop.

00:47:01.400 --> 00:47:02.920
See which one it is from the back here.

00:47:03.480 --> 00:47:04.940
It looks like an Andretti one to me.

00:47:05.080 --> 00:47:08.260
That's various members of the Andretti family swapping places.

00:47:08.460 --> 00:47:09.220
That's number 19.

00:47:09.560 --> 00:47:18.330
It's an interesting idea, in fact, driving with your son and your nephew.

00:47:18.470 --> 00:47:19.730
I think I couldn't have any sleep at all.

00:47:19.830 --> 00:47:21.850
I was worried about driving with my son and my nephew.

00:47:22.010 --> 00:47:25.950
Well, it's something he's very, very much wanted to achieve, is a family win.

00:47:26.220 --> 00:47:29.200
He said to me some years ago, after he'd won the World Championship,

00:47:29.340 --> 00:47:29.980
he won in Annapolis...

00:47:30.960 --> 00:47:36.060
championships, he'd won so much, and I said to him, Mario, anything left, anything you

00:47:36.060 --> 00:47:42.620
really want to do before you pack it? He said, yes, I want to win Le Mans with my son. Can

00:47:42.620 --> 00:47:46.360
he do it this time? He's not all that well placed at the moment, but there's a long time

00:47:46.360 --> 00:47:53.870
yet. Ah, yes, and they've driven their own race at their own pace. There is 1-2-1. That's

00:47:53.870 --> 00:47:59.260
Evan Clemens, Costas Lawson, Wayne Taylor in a C2 car, one of the spices.

00:48:04.320 --> 00:48:11.100
competitive field that it's mainly spices but works ones and private ones but built by the spice

00:48:11.190 --> 00:48:15.070
company but there are a lot of other contestants as well give them a hard time if they have any

00:48:15.070 --> 00:48:21.810
trouble in the context of the world sports prototype championship of course the more

00:48:21.810 --> 00:48:26.390
is different to any other race because most of those races are thousand kilometer races and

00:48:26.390 --> 00:48:29.900
they last anything up to five and a half six hours now here

00:48:30.160 --> 00:48:35.550
talking about four times that distance, which is 24 hours. And of course, as a result of

00:48:35.550 --> 00:48:41.830
that, you get double points if you win at Le Mans. But it is by itself perhaps the most

00:48:41.830 --> 00:48:46.590
important race in the championship. And I would beg to differ with anybody who said

00:48:46.590 --> 00:48:49.670
that it wasn't perhaps the most important race in the world. There are a couple of three

00:48:49.670 --> 00:48:53.510
sprint events in the championship as well, which tend to attract a lot of the German

00:48:53.510 --> 00:48:58.080
cars. But the big beauty of Le Mans is things like that fairground that we've just seen there,

00:48:58.080 --> 00:48:59.980
which everybody goes and visits, it's a special...

00:49:00.090 --> 00:49:03.040
Sure, day out, Anthony, isn't it? It's something like no other motor race.

00:49:03.160 --> 00:49:06.540
I think the funfair and the frolic in the forest is part of it.

00:49:06.880 --> 00:49:11.370
A lot of people may not be tremendous racing enthusiasts, but this is a happening.

00:49:11.570 --> 00:49:16.380
If they are racing enthusiasts, very often it pauls a bit, the race settles down.

00:49:16.500 --> 00:49:18.880
This doesn't look like doing it, but very often it does.

00:49:19.430 --> 00:49:24.670
But going back to what you were saying, I think to win Le Mans is extremely important for a big manufacturer.

00:49:25.830 --> 00:49:29.750
Jaguar last year, 1987, they won the championship, but they didn't win here.

00:49:30.440 --> 00:49:35.200
I think it's a tremendous challenge to them to win here again after 21 years.

00:49:35.950 --> 00:49:41.850
The commercial payoff for a team that wins Le Mans, a manufacturer's team, is tremendous.

00:49:41.910 --> 00:49:43.430
And I'm sure they want that.

00:49:43.570 --> 00:49:45.190
They got a great deal out of winning the championship,

00:49:45.190 --> 00:49:49.090
but I think Le Mans is a very, very special thing to win.

00:49:49.670 --> 00:49:51.900
You can see now that night is falling.

00:49:52.020 --> 00:49:55.230
The lights are coming into their own slowly but surely.

00:49:55.410 --> 00:49:59.440
On the far side of the circuit out here, you've just got the lights of the cars.

00:50:00.000 --> 00:50:05.260
this brightly lit pit area flash through it just like a flash and this of course goes on all night

00:50:05.260 --> 00:50:10.360
a lot of the spectators in fact are staying to watch this battle but now some of them move away

00:50:10.360 --> 00:50:15.330
into the mall itself to the hundreds of little catering sites around the circuit where you can

00:50:15.330 --> 00:50:20.210
get in fact some extremely good food i should tell you there is of course the normal hospitality now

00:50:20.210 --> 00:50:24.710
that goes on at large motor race meetings with most of the teams and many of the trade sponsors

00:50:24.710 --> 00:50:29.310
having hospitality units here, serving hundreds and thousands of meals to people.

00:50:30.730 --> 00:50:33.390
Like Ryan said, there's a church service even in the morning.

00:50:33.510 --> 00:50:39.420
The whole of Le Mans is geared, it's almost like a village that comes alive once every year for the 24 hours.

00:50:39.920 --> 00:50:43.660
And you see there, they've changed the nose cone on the front of that number 17 Porsche.

00:50:43.900 --> 00:50:45.460
I wonder whether he's been off somewhere.

00:50:46.860 --> 00:50:51.180
Well, we have just heard, in fact, that he did touch the back of another car.

00:50:51.340 --> 00:50:52.300
And that's the reason why.

00:50:53.030 --> 00:50:56.530
But you can see both lights are working where they weren't when he came into the pit.

00:50:56.710 --> 00:50:57.970
So that's the reason for that stop.

00:50:58.380 --> 00:50:59.580
It would have delayed him but little.

00:51:00.000 --> 00:51:06.390
got an infrared picture this again is technology moving into the 20th century the latter half

00:51:06.390 --> 00:51:11.330
this is what they see through infrared sites but this is an infrared picture of a car going

00:51:11.330 --> 00:51:14.670
around le mans we can actually pick up the car we can't pick up the number but it's quite a

00:51:14.670 --> 00:51:19.190
contrast with the normal sort of night shots that we have at le mans and here's one of those as we've

00:51:19.190 --> 00:51:23.770
got coming into the pits one of the jaguars number one the martin brundle john nielsen car it looks

00:51:23.770 --> 00:51:29.810
as though nielsen is about to take over from bruntle though a change of driver for the number one

00:51:30.800 --> 00:51:36.440
by just two people it's not leading the race it's up there now and of course if

00:51:36.440 --> 00:51:40.830
the Jaguars get into a fighting the strong fighting position they will tend to

00:51:40.830 --> 00:51:45.330
drive this race as something of a team race it was the one that was really

00:51:45.330 --> 00:51:50.540
intended I think to win if all things were equal well Anthony you know we've

00:51:50.540 --> 00:51:55.790
had nine hours of racing now because it's the witching hour it's midnight and

00:51:56.070 --> 00:51:59.910
it's still a ding-dong battle that's going really on the pit stops between

00:52:00.000 --> 00:52:05.800
Porsche and Jaguar. In the lead now, literally at midnight, number 18 Porsche, that's the Bob Wallach,

00:52:05.880 --> 00:52:11.360
the Saral van der Merwey, Verne Schoepen car in second place, Jaguar number two, Andy Wallace,

00:52:11.500 --> 00:52:16.940
Jan Lammers, Johnny Dumfries in third place, Porsche number 17, who's come up from that delay

00:52:16.940 --> 00:52:21.510
that we saw, the Stuck Bell Ludwig car, the one that went out of petrol, then number 21 Jaguar,

00:52:21.630 --> 00:52:26.760
the first of the American cars, Sullivan, Jones and Price Cobb, then number one Jaguar,

00:52:27.210 --> 00:52:29.770
That's the Brundle-Nielsen car. That was the other one that was delayed.

00:52:30.160 --> 00:52:33.460
Then number 19, the Andretti's Porsche, the Andretti family.

00:52:33.720 --> 00:52:36.500
And number 8 Porsche, the first of the non-works cars,

00:52:36.980 --> 00:52:39.880
one of the Blaupunkt Joost racing cars.

00:52:40.080 --> 00:52:44.560
And then finally the next Jaguar, the number 22 Jaguar, the silk-cut car.

00:52:44.720 --> 00:52:49.920
Sadly missing the number 3 car, the Pescarello, Raul Basel, John Watson car.

00:52:49.920 --> 00:52:54.370
That has stopped out on the circuit with what we are told is a transmission problem.

00:52:54.430 --> 00:52:59.970
But that's one Jaguar out, four Jaguars still in, still the three Porsches in.

00:53:00.540 --> 00:53:05.480
And one of the Jaguars in now, having more than I would say routine maintenance at this point.

00:53:05.890 --> 00:53:09.370
Yes, that looks like a problem. It's difficult to see exactly what they're doing.

00:53:09.590 --> 00:53:17.260
Of course, it is most unusual at Le Mans for what we used to call the Le Mans two-hour Grand Prix to go on so long.

00:53:17.530 --> 00:53:22.460
These cars are still racing mostly on the same lap, give or take a pit stop or two,

00:53:22.680 --> 00:53:28.440
and they're locked in combat just like a Grand Prix, and as you say, the race is nine hours old.

00:53:28.440 --> 00:53:29.760
It really is.

00:53:30.350 --> 00:53:35.710
tremendous fillet for both Jaguar and Porsche that this race should be going on, but particularly

00:53:35.710 --> 00:53:40.010
so for the spectators, all the British spectators that have come here, but a very rare thing

00:53:40.010 --> 00:53:44.740
indeed, and one tends to look back perhaps at the very early Le Mans, there's the Yost

00:53:44.740 --> 00:53:51.200
Porsche in, having what looks to be some fairly minor maintenance done on the car in this

00:53:51.200 --> 00:53:55.320
night's section, but I would say we tend to look back perhaps at the electrics, the enormous

00:53:55.440 --> 00:53:59.860
Reliance that people put now on the huge high-capacity lights of

00:54:00.600 --> 00:54:02.240
during the course of the hours of darkness.

00:54:02.940 --> 00:54:06.300
This is one of the reasons why Le Mans was so popular in the 20s,

00:54:06.380 --> 00:54:10.250
because it was responsible for the development of modern lighting

00:54:10.250 --> 00:54:11.930
that we see on cars today.

00:54:12.070 --> 00:54:13.470
Jaguar No. 2 coming in.

00:54:13.570 --> 00:54:15.290
This is the leading Jaguar.

00:54:15.430 --> 00:54:18.350
It's not leading the race at this moment, but it's the leading Jaguar.

00:54:18.840 --> 00:54:21.400
The No. 2 car, Lammers Dumfries Wallace.

00:54:22.140 --> 00:54:23.560
Now, is it all well, is it routine?

00:54:24.020 --> 00:54:25.300
Yes, that looks to be routine.

00:54:26.440 --> 00:54:29.480
You can see tucked down behind those huge plastic grills.

00:54:30.060 --> 00:54:33.220
Another light, and that's the number seven Porsche,

00:54:33.730 --> 00:54:38.030
the Juste Porsche, Didier Tay, Franz Conrad, David Hobb's car.

00:54:38.310 --> 00:54:41.410
And that seems to be stationary a little bit longer

00:54:41.410 --> 00:54:42.990
than perhaps we'd have expected at this stage,

00:54:43.070 --> 00:54:44.730
because he was about to go out and then changed his mind.

00:54:44.790 --> 00:54:45.570
Now he's off now.

00:54:45.820 --> 00:54:47.540
But when the works team dropped out,

00:54:47.700 --> 00:54:50.820
the Juste Porsches upheld the mark and won the race.

00:54:51.880 --> 00:54:53.980
Well, it's 8 a.m. in the morning now,

00:54:54.000 --> 00:54:57.670
and the position has changed a little during the night.

00:54:58.110 --> 00:54:59.970
It's changed because one Porsche

00:55:00.320 --> 00:55:05.830
number 18 Porsche and the number 19 car has dropped back with an engine problem

00:55:05.830 --> 00:55:10.310
but Jaguars currently are leading the race with the number two car the pace

00:55:10.310 --> 00:55:14.740
setter and then behind them having made up for all that lost time number 17

00:55:14.740 --> 00:55:19.310
Porsche that's the Derrick Bell Hans Tuch Klaus Ludwig car is up to second

00:55:19.310 --> 00:55:24.250
place and the number one Jaguar is up to third place as the tired spectators

00:55:24.250 --> 00:55:28.190
some of them who have stayed by the trackside not bother to get up to go to

00:55:28.190 --> 00:55:29.490
those services we talked about.

00:55:30.000 --> 00:55:36.020
the number one car the martin brandle john nielsen car come past the pit area here which is now

00:55:36.020 --> 00:55:42.020
beginning to fill up again people coming back to the circuit windscreens you will see cleaned or are

00:55:42.020 --> 00:55:48.490
in fact using the metal there to stop the misting up on the inside i thought we got a crack there

00:55:48.490 --> 00:55:54.040
because one of the jaguars this morning did have a windscreen change yes it did and i tell you what

00:55:54.040 --> 00:55:58.820
there's some problems in the porsche team the 18 cars went as you say and i think it was something

00:55:58.820 --> 00:55:59.960
to do with the cooling system.

00:56:00.030 --> 00:56:04.780
and the Andretti's have been very badly delayed, there's bits of car flying all over the place there, I don't know what that was.

00:56:04.780 --> 00:56:09.920
That's a Jaguar, and that I think was a tyre going on it, there's a Jaguar there that had a tyre go,

00:56:10.980 --> 00:56:16.640
so a dramatic moment for Jaguar, and we didn't see, or were able to pick up then, exactly which Jaguar that was.

00:56:16.760 --> 00:56:21.800
There's the number two car going through, the leading car, so it wasn't that, but one of the Jaguars has had a tyre go.

00:56:22.750 --> 00:56:25.210
It's the number one car. It's the number one car.

00:56:25.230 --> 00:56:29.310
It's Nielsen or Brundle, I think it's Nielsen coming in very slowly, as you say, with a blown tyre.

00:56:29.310 --> 00:56:29.980
but the

00:56:30.000 --> 00:56:34.360
Porsches, the Andrettes, have got intercooling problems as well

00:56:34.360 --> 00:56:37.040
and they've dropped back to about 7th or 8th place

00:56:37.970 --> 00:56:40.770
and I think Porsches are worried that this epidemic

00:56:40.770 --> 00:56:44.480
might attack their current frontrunner, number 17,

00:56:44.980 --> 00:56:48.740
which, of course, is the original Stuck-Bell-Ludwig car

00:56:48.950 --> 00:56:52.090
because, of course, Bell would love to bring this home

00:56:52.090 --> 00:56:55.190
and equal Jackie Eakes' all-time record.

00:56:55.880 --> 00:56:58.380
But, meanwhile, the Jaguars are going strongly,

00:56:58.580 --> 00:56:59.970
although number 20...

00:57:00.030 --> 00:57:04.820
22 has the second of the American cars is way, way back.

00:57:04.920 --> 00:57:06.160
No, number 21, I correct.

00:57:07.250 --> 00:57:10.770
21 is way, way back, down in 20-somethingth place.

00:57:11.230 --> 00:57:13.450
They've had transmission problems,

00:57:13.700 --> 00:57:17.420
which are roughly similar to what has been holding back

00:57:17.420 --> 00:57:19.380
the number one car on and off.

00:57:19.910 --> 00:57:22.350
Yes, the number 22 car, in fact, Anthony, you were right there,

00:57:22.450 --> 00:57:24.030
it had been quite a long way back.

00:57:24.130 --> 00:57:27.230
It's been driven at a much slower pace, in fact, than the other Jaguars,

00:57:27.270 --> 00:57:29.730
but it is now beginning to make its presence felt.

00:57:30.440 --> 00:57:32.620
The Jaguars have not had things all their own way,

00:57:32.660 --> 00:57:34.360
although they've been the most numerous of cars.

00:57:34.440 --> 00:57:37.630
The number eight Porsche you see there, that's the Joost car.

00:57:38.140 --> 00:57:40.620
That is in fourth place at the moment.

00:57:40.780 --> 00:57:46.190
Here's the number 17 Porsche, the leading Porsche of the three that started.

00:57:46.370 --> 00:57:50.150
Now, in fact, one of them out, so two remaining comes in for a scheduled stop.

00:57:50.270 --> 00:57:52.070
There's the number eight car in fourth place.

00:57:52.560 --> 00:57:55.090
And the battle now, the endurance battle,

00:57:55.110 --> 00:57:57.810
because it's so rare to have, of course, in Le Mans,

00:57:57.970 --> 00:57:59.970
when you often get a lead of 70, 80, 100.

00:58:00.060 --> 00:58:02.150
100 miles, the car's so close together,

00:58:02.230 --> 00:58:04.770
there's no more than the odd pit stop in it.

00:58:05.080 --> 00:58:07.840
This is going to go down as one of the great Le Mans in history.

00:58:07.980 --> 00:58:12.310
Whoever wins this night-long and now day-long battle

00:58:12.310 --> 00:58:13.590
between the two teams,

00:58:13.830 --> 00:58:18.130
now Porsche, depending entirely on the number 17 car,

00:58:18.520 --> 00:58:21.820
backed up by that number 8, Reinhold Joost, private team car.

00:58:22.280 --> 00:58:25.140
The Jaguars, with their numbers 2 and 1 at the moment in front,

00:58:25.900 --> 00:58:27.980
22s hanging thereabouts in fifth place,

00:58:28.140 --> 00:58:29.960
as I say, 21, the other American one...

00:58:30.190 --> 00:58:34.500
way back, the Andretti's in trouble, number three's gone, now number one's in trouble

00:58:34.500 --> 00:58:39.940
with that tire. So really, I think it's down to number two Jaguar and 17 Porsche, probably

00:58:39.940 --> 00:58:44.850
to fight this thing out. And as you will see from your picture, it's no longer quite as

00:58:44.850 --> 00:58:49.890
bright as it was yesterday when we started this race, and there is the threat all the

00:58:49.890 --> 00:58:54.150
time that we may have that one thing that Hans Stuck is waiting for, and that is rain.

00:58:54.270 --> 00:58:58.410
The rain master Stuck in the rain is something I think we'd all love to see. We've been told

00:58:58.410 --> 00:58:59.870
all the way through this weekend.

00:59:00.120 --> 00:59:04.120
that it's going to rain. It didn't rain through the night, which is what really makes things

00:59:04.120 --> 00:59:08.710
most difficult for people. Now, is it going to get through the whole race without rain?

00:59:09.400 --> 00:59:13.440
Well, Le Mans without rain is a pretty rare thing. You can see around the circuit there,

00:59:13.500 --> 00:59:18.820
there are cars littered about a little bit, but the basic reliability record of all the

00:59:18.820 --> 00:59:24.480
cars in this 1988 Le Mans 24-hour race has been pretty good. By any account, we've got

00:59:24.480 --> 00:59:29.580
a lot of cars left in, a lot of competitive cars left in, but never, ever, for a very

00:59:29.580 --> 00:59:29.840
long

00:59:30.380 --> 00:59:32.420
going back now perhaps to the 60s, I think,

00:59:32.500 --> 00:59:34.480
that famous race between Porsche and Ford,

00:59:34.500 --> 00:59:36.320
if we had cars so close together.

00:59:36.480 --> 00:59:38.460
And there's the leading Jaguar, the number two car,

00:59:38.890 --> 00:59:41.590
the car we saw start off making the pace for Jaguar.

00:59:41.670 --> 00:59:43.370
That's remarkable, you know, that car's still be here.

00:59:43.510 --> 00:59:45.710
That car's been the front-runner for Jaguars

00:59:45.710 --> 00:59:46.870
all the way through the race.

00:59:46.970 --> 00:59:49.230
It's been in the lead for many, many laps on the board,

00:59:49.370 --> 00:59:50.610
and this looks like trouble.

00:59:50.750 --> 00:59:54.470
This looks like the number one car, I think, is it?

00:59:54.720 --> 00:59:56.220
Yes, it's the number one car, Anthony.

00:59:56.380 --> 00:59:58.700
That looks very much to me like a cracked cylinder head,

00:59:58.780 --> 00:59:59.380
the big...

00:59:59.380 --> 00:59:59.520
Thank you.

01:00:00.060 --> 01:00:03.660
V12 cylinder head, if that goes, you've got a problem, and they've got one.

01:00:05.780 --> 01:00:06.580
Afraid they have.

01:00:06.780 --> 01:00:13.020
We're still, as I say, dependent on this car, Jaguar, and the number 17 car for Porsche.

01:00:13.320 --> 01:00:16.460
I think other people are in trouble of one kind and another,

01:00:16.980 --> 01:00:21.810
and, of course, we keep saying the Reinhold Joost car number 8 is still there,

01:00:22.230 --> 01:00:26.570
joining the Porsche team in the defence of Le Mans.

01:00:27.180 --> 01:00:29.980
Well, that car is moving up now to third place because...

01:00:30.090 --> 01:00:34.440
We now have confirmation that number one Jaguar has got a big problem.

01:00:34.760 --> 01:00:36.980
It is a cylinder head gasket that has gone.

01:00:37.080 --> 01:00:38.780
The cylinder head is cracked. It's out of the race.

01:00:38.860 --> 01:00:41.560
And here's the rain. The rain, as we feared, might happen.

01:00:41.700 --> 01:00:44.480
And this could change the whole thing around all over again.

01:00:44.680 --> 01:00:47.040
Well, of course, this puts us into tyre change tactics.

01:00:47.560 --> 01:00:52.440
It's very, very important not to give in too soon in case it's only a shower.

01:00:52.660 --> 01:00:56.560
When I say give in, I mean come in, put on the treaded rain tyres,

01:00:56.700 --> 01:00:58.040
which, of course, means you lap slower.

01:00:58.200 --> 01:00:59.980
It won't last very long if it dries.

01:01:00.760 --> 01:01:05.560
On the other hand, not to stay out too long and get the car unmanageable on the racing slick tyres.

01:01:05.680 --> 01:01:07.700
The number two leading Jaguar there.

01:01:07.960 --> 01:01:08.940
Now, what are they going to do?

01:01:09.100 --> 01:01:12.360
How long are they going to put up with the rain on these tyres?

01:01:12.680 --> 01:01:14.700
There are, of course, intermediate tyres.

01:01:14.900 --> 01:01:16.920
There's a sort of damp track tyre as well.

01:01:17.180 --> 01:01:22.180
But again, if it dries out, it limits your speed and you'd have to stop again to change them.

01:01:22.300 --> 01:01:29.910
The number 17 Porsche there, the Stuck Bell car fighting its way, trying to stave off.

01:01:30.030 --> 01:01:34.410
this jaguar challenge and you can see the tail of the car weaving there as they tried to put the

01:01:34.410 --> 01:01:39.700
power down on the wheel spin and it now is very slippery in places funnily enough as is often

01:01:39.700 --> 01:01:43.300
the case with a long circuit like this you've got more rain in some places than in others

01:01:43.300 --> 01:01:49.180
so it goes from varying conditions of being slippery very slippery and not so slippery now

01:01:49.180 --> 01:01:55.440
here's the jaguar in they've changed the driver which car is that because that might be the lead

01:01:55.440 --> 01:01:59.960
car going on to wet or intermediate tires which would be very important tactically well tactically

01:02:00.120 --> 01:02:03.920
will be, but also, tactically, from Porsche's point of view, this is when they will want

01:02:03.920 --> 01:02:07.940
Hans Stuck out, because it's at this stage of the race, there's the number 22 Jaguar,

01:02:08.140 --> 01:02:12.110
incidentally, that Stuck, the acknowledged Rainmaster, will come into his own.

01:02:12.580 --> 01:02:16.480
Yes, it's extraordinary how some drivers have this great genius on a wet track.

01:02:17.180 --> 01:02:23.230
There is those two cars together, the number 17 Porsche, the number 22 Jaguar, or they're

01:02:23.230 --> 01:02:25.730
not actually on the same lap, they're not together in order.

01:02:27.070 --> 01:02:29.330
But Jaguar number two leads.

01:02:29.330 --> 01:02:29.910
It's Jaguar.

01:02:30.060 --> 01:02:33.680
Number two leaves this 1988 Le Mans 24-hour race.

01:02:33.930 --> 01:02:38.620
Andy Wallace, Johnny Dumfries, Jan Lammers lead from the Stuck,

01:02:38.920 --> 01:02:41.240
Bell, Klaus Ludwig, Porsche number 17.

01:02:41.520 --> 01:02:43.040
And there's only seconds between them.

01:02:43.160 --> 01:02:46.220
Then it's back to car number eight, one of the private Porsches,

01:02:46.320 --> 01:02:49.160
the Blaupunkt cars of the ghost racing.

01:02:49.380 --> 01:02:51.840
Number 22, Jaguar in fourth place.

01:02:52.300 --> 01:02:54.280
One of the drivers there, the Australian, Larry Perkins.

01:02:54.700 --> 01:02:55.820
Then number seven, Porsche.

01:02:56.020 --> 01:02:57.620
One of the drivers there, Britain's David Hobbs.

01:02:58.080 --> 01:02:59.960
And back then to number 19, the Andretti.

01:03:00.000 --> 01:03:04.500
That is Porsche, the other surviving works car, the one that's running on five cylinders, not six.

01:03:04.600 --> 01:03:08.640
But you can see on the grass there, that is stuck on the grass, on dry tyres,

01:03:09.120 --> 01:03:12.780
struggling hard to stay out there on a damp and slippery track

01:03:12.780 --> 01:03:16.780
and to catch up with the Jaguar Challenge, and there are seconds between them.

01:03:17.180 --> 01:03:20.920
I think it's going to be who makes the right decision about tyres,

01:03:21.060 --> 01:03:22.540
gambling on what the weather's going to do.

01:03:23.060 --> 01:03:28.460
That's going to be a big advantage in this race if one or other of them makes a different decision.

01:03:28.680 --> 01:03:29.980
and astonishingly

01:03:30.160 --> 01:03:34.720
Stuck is going on past. That's amazing, after he's found how difficult it is.

01:03:35.580 --> 01:03:40.120
You know, we talk about Grand Prix drivers and the difference between Grand Prix drivers and long-distance drivers,

01:03:40.240 --> 01:03:44.660
but that difference is getting less and less. There are so many drivers here who are current Grand Prix drivers,

01:03:44.820 --> 01:03:47.300
just ex-Grand Prix drivers, like Brundle, who's taken a year off,

01:03:47.760 --> 01:03:53.000
Wallace, who is obviously going towards Grand Prix racing, Dumfries, who could quite easily do so,

01:03:53.320 --> 01:03:59.540
Lammers, who has. It's now no longer the province of, shall we say, a driver who's not got quite the pace and Stuck.

01:04:00.030 --> 01:04:03.610
Well, let's face it, if he was a smaller man, he'd fit in a Grand Prix car.

01:04:03.710 --> 01:04:07.110
But because he's six-foot-plus tall, to fit him in a current Grand Prix car, virtually impossible.

01:04:07.350 --> 01:04:10.430
Now, there's the leader. Still the spray. I think the rain's easing off a bit.

01:04:11.600 --> 01:04:18.050
The Reinhold Josporce, number eight now, a fourth-place car, a third-place car, I think, now, in the pits.

01:04:18.840 --> 01:04:22.760
Now, what sort of tyres are they putting on? Can we see or is that man going to stand in the way?

01:04:22.860 --> 01:04:28.450
He's going to stand in the way. So I don't know whether they put dry-weather tyres back on again or not.

01:04:29.050 --> 01:04:29.690
There's the leader.

01:04:30.060 --> 01:04:35.100
The race leader, that car which has led so many laps and so many hours of this race,

01:04:35.240 --> 01:04:40.070
that number two Jaguar, we talked about it being the hair sent out to break the others

01:04:40.070 --> 01:04:44.120
and perhaps get broken, but in fact, two of the other Jaguars have fallen by the wayside,

01:04:44.780 --> 01:04:49.590
one of the Porsches and another one's in trouble, so it's that car which has been fighting

01:04:49.590 --> 01:04:54.350
for the lead all the way, that number two and the number 17 Porsche, they're still fighting

01:04:54.370 --> 01:04:54.750
it out.

01:04:56.110 --> 01:04:59.980
And we're looking at the Porsche, the one that had that tremendous...

01:05:00.160 --> 01:05:06.270
moment that we saw a few minutes ago, and now at the Jaguar, and it's between these two cars that

01:05:06.270 --> 01:05:14.740
we have had this battle going on race-long. Track dried out now, spray's gone. We're over three-quarters

01:05:14.740 --> 01:05:22.350
distance in this 1988 Le Mans 24-hour race, and it was in 1957 that Jaguar won this race last. Prior

01:05:22.350 --> 01:05:29.970
to that, 56, then 55, they finished second in 54, they won again in 53, they all retired in 52, and won for

01:05:30.190 --> 01:05:36.500
first time in 1951, but Porsche, well, their record is the one of win after win that they're

01:05:36.500 --> 01:05:42.260
defending at the moment. Yes, and of course, they're only racing in Le Mans at this year's

01:05:42.260 --> 01:05:46.960
series, this year's championship. The works Porsche team have come out of a hibernation

01:05:46.960 --> 01:05:52.120
just for Le Mans, whereas the Jaguars, of course, are doing the whole series, and the

01:05:52.120 --> 01:05:57.690
Reinhold Joost team and on and off the Kramer team are doing most of the races. Yes, the

01:05:57.690 --> 01:05:59.570
Porsche made its debut at Le Mans.

01:06:00.090 --> 01:06:03.890
Those days they were called the 956, but there's not a great deal of difference.

01:06:04.490 --> 01:06:09.810
The basic design is the same, and they have won Le Mans every year since that car first entered.

01:06:10.050 --> 01:06:15.090
And this looks as if it might be the time when they might be beaten by a British car

01:06:15.090 --> 01:06:17.730
that hasn't won this race for 31 years.

01:06:19.840 --> 01:06:24.080
That, I think, is the Andretti car ahead, although several laps behind,

01:06:24.340 --> 01:06:28.950
of the leading car in the race number two, Jaguar.

01:06:29.240 --> 01:06:29.760
Are we getting it?

01:06:30.000 --> 01:06:34.870
Getting the situation now where you notice, Neville, every time the Jaguar has a pit stop,

01:06:35.480 --> 01:06:37.960
the number 17 Porsche begins to catch up a bit.

01:06:38.700 --> 01:06:42.160
And then, of course, he has his pit stop and the Jaguar pulls away.

01:06:43.060 --> 01:06:44.500
Yes, they're keeping that gap.

01:06:44.640 --> 01:06:51.390
And the gap, after nearly 24 hours, is still this amazing minute, minute and a half, something like that.

01:06:51.510 --> 01:06:54.230
It's quite unbelievable, a historic Le Mans race.

01:06:54.580 --> 01:06:58.140
It says a tremendous amount for the concentration and strength of the drivers

01:06:58.140 --> 01:06:59.980
and, of course, for the quality of the...

01:07:00.440 --> 01:07:04.460
they can stand this high-speed challenge all the way through,

01:07:04.620 --> 01:07:07.060
because it's not usual at the moment, it's not what anybody expects.

01:07:07.200 --> 01:07:09.900
Usually, at some stage, somebody's way way ahead,

01:07:10.120 --> 01:07:13.040
somebody's dropped back, and you can back off and ease off just a little.

01:07:13.180 --> 01:07:15.260
But they've been racing right up against the limit.

01:07:15.480 --> 01:07:19.040
It's interesting also, so far, we haven't seen any sign of them

01:07:19.040 --> 01:07:20.740
having to back off because of fuel shortage.

01:07:21.440 --> 01:07:24.180
No, the fuel situation, in fact, we've been peeping

01:07:24.180 --> 01:07:25.980
when we had a chance to get out into the pits

01:07:25.980 --> 01:07:27.760
to see how things are going there,

01:07:27.800 --> 01:07:29.980
and you can see there as the Jaguar...

01:07:30.350 --> 01:07:34.960
off the final lap of the race, the excitement as he comes into the pits for the last time.

01:07:35.100 --> 01:07:40.780
And this is the important pit stop as far as Jaguar are concerned. Change of wheels,

01:07:41.100 --> 01:07:46.620
they're inspecting the brake pads. Here a race can be won or lost at this stage in the pits and

01:07:46.620 --> 01:07:51.800
they'll give this car a really good check over before it goes out with mounting excitement now

01:07:51.800 --> 01:07:58.260
here at Le Mans with Jaguar well set for a historic victory. But before they can win that

01:07:58.260 --> 01:07:59.980
victory, they've got to survive the last

01:08:00.060 --> 01:08:05.130
around its problems in the past well this of course is where the tension mounts for everybody

01:08:05.130 --> 01:08:09.830
all the jaguar fans all around the track who want them to win the drivers who worked so hard the

01:08:09.830 --> 01:08:15.510
mechanics of the team who've worked so hard and everything at that car has been used to the

01:08:15.510 --> 01:08:21.760
ultimate all the way through now for nearly 24 hours and it must be showing the strain that what a

01:08:21.760 --> 01:08:29.040
couple of its teammates have gone out with troubles and this is as you say its last planned routine

01:08:29.040 --> 01:08:29.940
pit stop that's

01:08:30.060 --> 01:08:35.020
hope it is the last pit stop because if it stops again that'll mean a problem and the same token

01:08:35.020 --> 01:08:41.090
the Porsche team are worried about that car. As we said before the other two cars had intercooling

01:08:41.090 --> 01:08:47.070
problems, cooling system problems and they're worried about the integrity of the number 17

01:08:47.070 --> 01:08:53.450
car's system so and this car has not had any problems except for a screen change I believe

01:08:53.450 --> 01:08:58.390
in the middle of the night so in a sense its track record literally is better than the other one

01:08:58.390 --> 01:08:59.670
but that doesn't mean

01:09:00.190 --> 01:09:04.270
the excitement's over because it isn't and seldom have we had a Le Mans where the excitement's

01:09:04.270 --> 01:09:09.150
kept up to so near the end. Yes I asked Tom Walkinshaw when he started preparing for this

01:09:09.150 --> 01:09:13.900
race and he said immediately after the last one indeed there is hours and hours of preparation

01:09:13.900 --> 01:09:19.600
of meetings of looking at every single component in the car to make quite sure that it will last

01:09:19.600 --> 01:09:25.080
the 24 hours and while they're doing that and racing of course in the championship as you've

01:09:25.080 --> 01:09:29.720
said Porsche have had a whole year to concentrate on this race and this race alone and

01:09:30.090 --> 01:09:34.350
They've obviously spent a lot of time on preparing these cars with now just one hour to go.

01:09:34.610 --> 01:09:39.590
And that's going to be one of the most nervous hours that anybody can ever expect to have to put up with at Le Mans.

01:09:40.140 --> 01:09:45.000
Well, it is, because if anybody has a problem now, it will be too late to make it up.

01:09:45.910 --> 01:09:51.430
It looks as though they're both able to run pretty competitively still at this late stage on their fuel allowance.

01:09:51.820 --> 01:09:54.820
So the fuel battle seems to have come out even.

01:09:55.400 --> 01:09:58.700
Yes, it only wants a puncture though, or something like that, something comparatively minor.

01:09:58.900 --> 01:09:59.980
And we had one of those on a jet.

01:10:00.920 --> 01:10:04.140
or he hit something or shredded a tower on some wreckage or something.

01:10:04.710 --> 01:10:08.110
There's the leading car, the Jaguar No. 2, leading the race.

01:10:09.500 --> 01:10:12.020
And so often, Le Mans has finished up, as we've said,

01:10:12.140 --> 01:10:13.900
with a huge distance between the cars,

01:10:14.020 --> 01:10:16.600
or a very sick car limping across the line.

01:10:17.040 --> 01:10:19.740
La Gonda won it but once, and the bearings had gone on the car,

01:10:19.820 --> 01:10:21.140
and they nursed it across the line.

01:10:21.340 --> 01:10:24.260
There was the heartbreak of Pierre Levesque in 1952,

01:10:24.340 --> 01:10:25.750
when he was so tired.

01:10:25.830 --> 01:10:28.890
In those days, drivers used to go right the way through the 24 hours without a change,

01:10:28.910 --> 01:10:29.970
and he...

01:10:30.350 --> 01:10:35.740
change in the gearbox because he changed from third to second instead of third to fourth and

01:10:35.740 --> 01:10:40.970
blew the engine sky high and all those things can happen in this last hour this wearying hour at

01:10:40.970 --> 01:10:45.230
Le Mans when the nerves the tempers and everything else begin to fray. Of course we were talking

01:10:45.230 --> 01:10:50.210
about the 1987 Le Mans which Jaguar didn't win although they won the championship but we mustn't

01:10:50.210 --> 01:10:55.440
forget that they did actually run in 86 and Tom Walkinshaw has been quoted as saying it would take

01:10:55.440 --> 01:10:59.640
them three years to make sure of it. Is this going to be the third year?

01:11:00.570 --> 01:11:02.190
the third year, but is he going to prove the point?

01:11:03.520 --> 01:11:05.700
First and fourth places for Jaguar.

01:11:06.310 --> 01:11:08.470
It was, of course, in 1957

01:11:08.470 --> 01:11:10.420
that they finished first,

01:11:10.660 --> 01:11:12.610
second, fourth, I think fifth and

01:11:12.610 --> 01:11:14.550
sixth or something like that, so they haven't done quite

01:11:14.550 --> 01:11:16.330
as well as they did then, but

01:11:16.330 --> 01:11:18.550
don't forget they've had but two cars

01:11:18.550 --> 01:11:20.490
of the five retired. There are three Jaguars still

01:11:20.490 --> 01:11:22.700
running. The number 22 car considerably

01:11:22.700 --> 01:11:24.530
back in the field. I think he's about

01:11:24.530 --> 01:11:26.050
eighth or ninth position at the moment.

01:11:26.310 --> 01:11:28.770
In fourth place, the other car,

01:11:28.890 --> 01:11:29.970
the other Jaguar, I think I might have

01:11:30.030 --> 01:11:30.990
We've got those the other way around.

01:11:31.170 --> 01:11:33.730
It's 22 that is, in fact, in fourth place,

01:11:33.790 --> 01:11:35.290
and it's 21 that's run further back, is it?

01:11:35.370 --> 01:11:38.150
Way further back, yes, way down in the teen places.

01:11:38.610 --> 01:11:41.530
They're number 21, that's Sullivan, Jones and Cobb.

01:11:41.590 --> 01:11:44.910
But Cogan, Daley, Perkins, yes, they're running fourth.

01:11:45.050 --> 01:11:48.910
They are, in fact, sandwiched between the two Reinholds Porsches,

01:11:48.950 --> 01:11:51.530
numbers eight and seven, which are third and fifth.

01:11:52.300 --> 01:11:53.500
Disappointingly for the Andrettis,

01:11:53.890 --> 01:11:56.940
they are sixth after their cooling problems,

01:11:56.960 --> 01:11:59.980
so Mario's got to try all over again if he really wants to...

01:12:00.000 --> 01:12:05.390
win the race with his son. But up front, that car there, number two, leads the Le Mans race

01:12:05.390 --> 01:12:13.730
for 1988, ahead of the number 17 Porsche of the great Le Mans experts, Stuck, Bell and

01:12:13.730 --> 01:12:19.460
Ludwig. You could hardly have a hotter Le Mans team than that lot. No, on paper, they've

01:12:19.460 --> 01:12:23.880
got all the experience, and if you look at Dumfries, or you look for that matter at Andy

01:12:23.880 --> 01:12:29.970
Wallace, they haven't really got the experience of Le Mans. You mustn't count your cats before

01:12:30.220 --> 01:12:36.100
the kittens are hatched, or whatever you say, but if they win, what a great thing for those

01:12:36.100 --> 01:12:39.580
two drivers, particularly, I mean, Jan Lammers hasn't won Le Mans either, but he has won

01:12:39.580 --> 01:12:45.800
a lot of races. Those two, Dumfries and Wallace, at the beginning end of their careers, what

01:12:45.800 --> 01:12:51.420
a great thing for them. Well, it's very important, in fact, I think, for Wallace, who's about

01:12:51.420 --> 01:12:58.310
to make his move into 3,000. Here comes the Jaguar No. 2 in the pits. This must be just

01:12:58.310 --> 01:12:59.970
It's a final top-up unless it's a...

01:13:00.570 --> 01:13:06.600
Yep, final top-up and the final decision as to who will drive that last heart-stopping hour.

01:13:07.900 --> 01:13:12.420
Yes, who's going to have the A, honour, B, burden of responsibility of bringing it in?

01:13:13.120 --> 01:13:17.340
I think it'll probably be Jan Lammers. He started the car and I suspect he'll finish the car.

01:13:18.210 --> 01:13:21.690
Certainly the most experienced, the senior member of that group, that team.

01:13:22.780 --> 01:13:27.220
Meanwhile, the Porsche goes on its way, catching up, catching up all the time,

01:13:27.340 --> 01:13:29.980
the Jagged in the pits, which wasn't long, just a final...

01:13:31.020 --> 01:13:35.740
not a full pit stop, maybe not much more than a driver change to put Lermas back in the car.

01:13:37.740 --> 01:13:40.480
All right, comes number two, and you can hear the cheer of the crowd there,

01:13:40.680 --> 01:13:43.640
the huge, massive crowd that have come back,

01:13:43.720 --> 01:13:46.870
those people who have travelled back during the course of the morning,

01:13:47.240 --> 01:13:49.620
some stayed in a hotel, some stepped late in the cars.

01:13:49.880 --> 01:13:51.820
It's all part of this wonderful atmosphere of Le Mans,

01:13:52.260 --> 01:13:55.860
and the beautiful part about it is, of course, the traffic at Le Mans all disposes of itself,

01:13:55.920 --> 01:13:59.900
as the Jaguars now are beginning to collect themselves together for a team.

01:14:00.250 --> 01:14:05.670
finish what how great it would be if they managed if they had the spare time as it were in hand over

01:14:05.670 --> 01:14:11.410
the Porsche to line themselves up for a three-car finish because there are three still go as we know

01:14:11.410 --> 01:14:16.460
number 21 Jaguar is well back in the field but if they could somehow get themselves into line

01:14:16.670 --> 01:14:20.530
radio control of one thing and another and come over the line together how great that would be

01:14:20.530 --> 01:14:25.520
but there's still time for things to go wrong before that happened well I'm looking we've

01:14:25.520 --> 01:14:29.980
got two of them running together now the first and fourth place car fourth place

01:14:30.120 --> 01:14:35.610
car uh interesting in that it has among its drivers larry perkins the australian driver who

01:14:35.610 --> 01:14:42.790
is representing twr in his other hat because they are busily preparing not only to race with jaguars

01:14:42.790 --> 01:14:49.900
but also with holdens the australian car again part of twr's uh business for the years and you

01:14:49.900 --> 01:14:53.620
can hear the cheers of the crowd and the waves of the flags as they come past the bits and this is

01:14:53.620 --> 01:14:57.360
happening every lap they're cheering them on towards victory the atmosphere is building up

01:14:57.360 --> 01:14:59.860
Now this is building to be the f...

01:15:00.090 --> 01:15:04.310
finish of a very fine Grand Prix, about 12 minutes to go only, about 12 minutes to go.

01:15:04.650 --> 01:15:09.680
You've got two of the Jaguars in line. Unless something goes wrong, the Porsche can't possibly

01:15:09.680 --> 01:15:13.860
catch them now. He's just got too much of a deficit. He kept getting off the lap when

01:15:13.860 --> 01:15:18.060
he stopped at the pits, and then the Jaguar stopped. He got back on the lap, but he couldn't

01:15:18.060 --> 01:15:24.150
make it up. So if this number two car keeps rolling as it is now, it's going to win. And

01:15:24.150 --> 01:15:29.610
it's got its number 22 teammate in fourth place behind it, the number 21 car, much

01:15:29.610 --> 01:15:29.890
further

01:15:30.350 --> 01:15:31.590
Three Jaguars still going.

01:15:32.260 --> 01:15:35.060
The question is whether they now can find the other one

01:15:35.060 --> 01:15:37.320
and get it lined up for a three-car finish.

01:15:38.120 --> 01:15:40.680
Yes, it's a question, really, of surviving this last, what,

01:15:40.840 --> 01:15:42.320
three laps or so, something like that,

01:15:42.380 --> 01:15:44.760
because one problem, it's still only seconds,

01:15:44.820 --> 01:15:49.010
and it's a real, oh, I don't know, curl the toes, the fingers, the lot.

01:15:49.110 --> 01:15:50.730
A jittery moment for everybody here.

01:15:50.910 --> 01:15:54.850
We're waiting to see whether or not this historic win can happen at Le Mans.

01:15:55.230 --> 01:15:57.870
The cars have run out of fuel in the last stages before now.

01:15:59.020 --> 01:15:59.980
I think he's going to win.

01:16:00.120 --> 01:16:02.500
I think he's going to do it, but we mustn't be too optimistic.

01:16:02.760 --> 01:16:07.760
You can so easily talk yourself into a state of joy, which is then taken from you.

01:16:09.620 --> 01:16:12.040
Two Jaguars running together, nose and tail.

01:16:12.360 --> 01:16:16.360
I don't know whether they'll actually manage to find the third one on the road in these final stages,

01:16:16.460 --> 01:16:17.600
or is that it behind?

01:16:17.860 --> 01:16:18.780
Great fun if they did.

01:16:19.130 --> 01:16:21.130
I think that might be the car behind, in fact, Anthony.

01:16:21.270 --> 01:16:23.630
I think you'll find that the three Jaguars have lined up,

01:16:23.730 --> 01:16:28.620
so it shows that whatever was the wrong with the American Jaguar that slowed,

01:16:28.680 --> 01:16:29.960
it's caught up again, it's going...

01:16:30.030 --> 01:16:34.680
well as ever, and all three cars now sounding healthy, sounding in fact very little different

01:16:34.680 --> 01:16:39.020
to when they started the race, have lined themselves up as we come into the last two

01:16:39.200 --> 01:16:45.940
laps of this 1988 Le Mans, and it looks as if Jaguar and Tom Wilkinshaw have been able

01:16:45.940 --> 01:16:50.560
to keep that promise that they made when they first competed three years ago.

01:16:50.760 --> 01:16:58.190
And they've got them together in 1st, 4th and 16th places, numbers 2, 22 and 21.

01:17:07.450 --> 01:17:11.430
raving spare now with excitement is what they came to see what they hope to see

01:17:11.430 --> 01:17:19.560
last year and they didn't you are I'm not gonna be the disappointing factor now

01:17:19.560 --> 01:17:22.740
at Le Mans they're all waving the flags look at the marshals waving all the

01:17:22.740 --> 01:17:26.920
flags as they come round to the finish this is a race incidentally which stops

01:17:26.920 --> 01:17:29.710
right at the finish there's no question of cooling off

01:17:30.440 --> 01:17:34.230
The crowd simply swarm all over the track as soon as the chequered flag comes out.

01:17:35.300 --> 01:17:40.530
If they miss a lap, if they miss going over before three o'clock, they have to go round again.

01:17:40.960 --> 01:17:45.550
And the marshals know, the crowd know, the drivers must know.

01:17:45.990 --> 01:17:51.250
It's just minutes to go now before the end of the 1988 Le Mans 24-hour race

01:17:51.250 --> 01:17:56.800
and Jaguars in line-ahead formation are making their way down the Maussan Strait

01:17:56.800 --> 01:17:59.680
for what will probably be the last time in this historic...

01:18:00.320 --> 01:18:05.690
effort towards victory for 1988 they would certainly want to make it the last time they

01:18:05.690 --> 01:18:11.920
wouldn't want as i say to cross over the line say at one minute or 40 seconds before three o'clock

01:18:11.920 --> 01:18:14.820
then they have to go all the way around again and they're at risk of things going wrong again

01:18:18.170 --> 01:18:25.630
marshals at there waving vigorously to the three jaguar drivers jaguar a very popular name

01:18:25.630 --> 01:18:29.970
of course at the moment it's been a long time since they gave their supporters this

01:18:30.960 --> 01:18:34.220
They're pit-singling at Mulsanne.

01:18:34.550 --> 01:18:36.630
Champagne already in the pit-singling area.

01:18:37.110 --> 01:18:39.810
Well, they've decided that Jaguar have won.

01:18:40.030 --> 01:18:42.490
The crowd have decided that Jaguar have won.

01:18:42.690 --> 01:18:44.690
And I think even now, Sir John Egan,

01:18:44.850 --> 01:18:46.670
who's been here to watch this race,

01:18:46.690 --> 01:18:49.150
to provide encouragement for the teams throughout,

01:18:49.840 --> 01:18:53.820
has decided that at last this victory has come true.

01:18:54.000 --> 01:18:55.420
For him, for Tom Walkinshaw,

01:18:55.520 --> 01:18:58.380
and all the team who've been competing here at Le Mans,

01:18:58.610 --> 01:18:59.970
it looks as if they've...

01:19:01.470 --> 01:19:05.530
And, of course, there's a big, big party of Jaguar guests here who've come to see just this.

01:19:05.940 --> 01:19:07.400
There's the second-place car.

01:19:07.500 --> 01:19:14.360
Don't forget that the number 17 Porsche of Stuck, Bell and Ludwig is still in second place and uncatchable.

01:19:15.140 --> 01:19:22.440
And in third place is the Reinhold Juss Porsche, number eight of Winter, Jelinski and Dickens.

01:19:22.600 --> 01:19:27.260
So that although Jaguars have won, Porsches have got second and third.

01:19:28.710 --> 01:19:29.970
Yes, by no means have bought...

01:19:30.060 --> 01:19:33.100
but I don't think they would have wanted it any other way, would they, Anthony?

01:19:33.440 --> 01:19:35.940
No, you don't want to... If you do that, it looks too easy.

01:19:36.060 --> 01:19:39.120
This has been hard, very difficult to do, and they've done it,

01:19:39.240 --> 01:19:40.300
and that's very important.

01:19:41.240 --> 01:19:43.860
The helicopter shot overhead as we look down,

01:19:44.020 --> 01:19:47.020
going to a camera still fairly high up,

01:19:47.180 --> 01:19:49.340
watching the cars, watching the marshals as they come round,

01:19:49.400 --> 01:19:50.300
and is this going to be it?

01:19:50.680 --> 01:19:54.460
Is this going to be the last lap of the 1988 Le Mans 24-hour race?

01:19:55.250 --> 01:19:57.830
It looks to me as if they might be a little bit premature,

01:19:58.440 --> 01:19:59.620
but we'll have to wait and see.

01:20:00.730 --> 01:20:08.450
All the flags are going, the crowd is surging forward, they've climbed what appeared to me to be impregnable barriers to get across the road.

01:20:08.510 --> 01:20:19.700
And it's only when we come into the pit area here that we'll see whether or not that massive crowd are going to mean that this is the last lap of Le Mans as the Jaguars come into the pit area.

01:20:20.020 --> 01:20:21.340
Checkered flag, they've done it.

01:20:22.060 --> 01:20:27.120
Jaguars have won the 1988 Le Mans 24-hour race which they set out to do.

01:20:27.490 --> 01:20:29.970
And they've brought great joy to the huge...

01:20:30.280 --> 01:20:36.500
contingent in this vast crowd. Many, many people have come over. We're told 50,000 British spectators

01:20:36.500 --> 01:20:42.220
here amongst the enormous crowd of French spectators and German and Italians and everyone else. A lot

01:20:42.220 --> 01:20:47.990
of American people here too. But I think it's fair to say this is a win for the Brits and they're

01:20:47.990 --> 01:20:52.360
going to enjoy it. They're flooding the track. The cars of course have to be stopped. Nobody can go

01:20:52.360 --> 01:20:58.130
around again. The cars coming in behind the Jaguars have got to be stopped so they don't rush

01:20:58.130 --> 01:20:59.890
into the crowd but it's a great

01:21:00.220 --> 01:21:05.180
and will bring great prestige to the name of Jaguar they tried the last two

01:21:05.180 --> 01:21:09.720
years they didn't make it this time they've done it and that number two car

01:21:09.720 --> 01:21:16.270
Lammers Dumfries and Wallace has led far and away the majority of the laps of the

01:21:16.270 --> 01:21:20.730
race this year we saw our last shot of the straight there that will soon be

01:21:20.730 --> 01:21:25.360
public road again as many of the roads of the Le Mans circuit are and the French

01:21:25.360 --> 01:21:29.760
of course I think delighted at a British victory it's always been a little bit

01:21:29.760 --> 01:21:29.960
of all-

01:21:30.700 --> 01:21:35.100
And if they can't win themselves, I think they'd as soon as see the British win as anybody

01:21:35.100 --> 01:21:41.620
because it has been years of Porsche domination, but a marvellous drive by this car with, of

01:21:41.620 --> 01:21:45.400
course, Derrick Bell, Klaus van Vick-Hanstook putting up a tremendous battle.

01:21:45.880 --> 01:21:50.360
This was no walk over victory here at Le Mans in 1988 for Jaguar.

01:21:50.520 --> 01:21:55.400
It was against Porsche who had devoted the whole of a year to preparing for this race.

01:21:55.860 --> 01:21:59.980
And now the question is, with 1988 won and in the bag, can...

01:22:00.190 --> 01:22:01.980
come back and do it again

01:22:01.980 --> 01:22:04.120
at Le Mans, because for all

01:22:04.120 --> 01:22:06.350
the Jaguar fans, for the fans

01:22:06.350 --> 01:22:08.420
of the world prototype sports

01:22:08.570 --> 01:22:10.690
racing, this is important

01:22:10.690 --> 01:22:12.710
that we have all these marks. There you see

01:22:12.710 --> 01:22:14.630
the winning drivers, Jan Lammers nearest the

01:22:14.630 --> 01:22:16.130
camera, Johnny Dumfries in the middle,

01:22:16.780 --> 01:22:18.540
on the far side there, Andy Wallace

01:22:18.540 --> 01:22:21.180
absolutely delighted with victory.

01:22:22.040 --> 01:22:23.240
Andy Wallace, it's his

01:22:23.240 --> 01:22:25.200
first major win, Benoit Froge, the

01:22:25.200 --> 01:22:27.180
public relations and press officer

01:22:27.180 --> 01:22:29.220
here for the Automobile Club of the West, is

01:22:29.220 --> 01:22:29.960
handing the drivers.

01:22:30.060 --> 01:22:32.060
hats, and they will no doubt throw

01:22:32.060 --> 01:22:33.540
them down to the crowd, Anthony.

01:22:34.160 --> 01:22:35.780
Yes, well, this is a great tradition, of course.

01:22:35.980 --> 01:22:37.320
They're supposed to wear these hats because

01:22:37.320 --> 01:22:40.380
the tire manufacturer would like them to, but they are

01:22:40.380 --> 01:22:42.460
a gift to the first chap who manages to catch them.

01:22:42.960 --> 01:22:44.600
Oh, Jan Lammers

01:22:44.600 --> 01:22:46.080
is wearing his.

01:22:47.000 --> 01:22:48.570
They've seen it together

01:22:48.570 --> 01:22:50.590
through the day and the night, and now

01:22:50.590 --> 01:22:52.570
it's their turn to celebrate, and they're fully

01:22:52.570 --> 01:22:54.830
entitled to it. And so are the mechanics,

01:22:55.290 --> 01:22:56.860
the engineers, the team

01:22:56.860 --> 01:22:58.940
chiefs, Tom Walkinshaw and his outfit

01:22:58.940 --> 01:22:59.980
who've brought this to general.

01:23:01.370 --> 01:23:12.750
There's the champagne, the champagne at the end of this race, and nobody's going to mind too much getting wet from that bottle of champagne that's being shaken.

01:23:13.010 --> 01:23:14.770
Let's just watch the cork, see where it goes.

01:23:14.790 --> 01:23:19.360
You can see the huge crowd below here who have come to celebrate this victory.

01:23:19.540 --> 01:23:22.340
There have been Jaguar flags, Jaguar umbrellas.

01:23:22.540 --> 01:23:29.550
The French, in fact, have really made their mark here by all the advertisements that you see in and around and on the way to the circuit.

01:23:30.000 --> 01:23:35.840
pictures of the Jaguars and there's the champagne flowing and that lady the girl there with Johnny

01:23:35.840 --> 01:23:42.220
Dumfries is his wife and she has been here to watch the race there is Tom Wilkinshaw the man

01:23:42.220 --> 01:23:47.830
who put the team together the Scotsman winner himself of the European Touring Car Championship

01:23:47.830 --> 01:23:53.480
he's going to compete in again later on this year but Tom delighted with the result here of Le Mans

01:23:53.480 --> 01:23:59.750
this last look then at the victorious Jaguars on what has become a historic occasion

01:24:00.000 --> 01:24:07.310
Le Mans 1988 will go down in history as being the time when Jaguars came back and won.

01:24:07.470 --> 01:24:14.380
For me, it's been a magnificent opportunity to see perhaps one of my ambitions to see Jaguars win here at Le Mans.

01:24:15.250 --> 01:24:19.570
and for you Anthony with a long experience of motor racing it must bring a lump to your throat as well.

01:24:19.750 --> 01:24:27.030
Indeed it does as the national anthem rings out to celebrate the victory of these three great drivers and a great team.

01:24:27.130 --> 01:24:29.790
Sir John Egan there enjoying the result.

01:24:30.510 --> 01:24:33.390
what he has put together with Tom Walkenshaw.

01:24:34.920 --> 01:24:38.420
And so it's time now for my colleague Anthony Marsh and I, Neville Hay,

01:24:38.660 --> 01:24:40.420
to leave Le Mans for another year.

01:24:40.800 --> 01:24:43.240
We have seen this historic Jaguar victory.

01:24:43.660 --> 01:24:47.200
We shall be back again to see if they can do it in 1989.
